🧱 I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
📌 Definition

👉 Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.

In simple words:

Don’t make a class implement things it doesn’t need.

🧠 Think Like a 10-Year-Old

Imagine a remote control 🎮

It has buttons for TV, AC, Lights, Fan, Music… EVERYTHING 😵

But you only want to control the TV

👉 Why should you deal with all other buttons?

So instead:

Have a separate remote for each device

👉 Same idea in coding:
Small, specific interfaces are better than one big interface

❌ Bad Example (Violating ISP)
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

Now:

class Robot implements Worker {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Robot working");
    }

    public void eat() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Robot doesn't eat");
    }
}
🚨 Problem:
Robot does not need eat()
But still forced to implement it

❌ Violates ISP

✅ Good Example (Following ISP)
Step 1: Split interfaces
interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}
Step 2: Implement only what is needed
class Human implements Workable, Eatable {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Human working");
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Human eating");
    }
}

class Robot implements Workable {
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Robot working");
    }
}
✅ Now:
Human → works + eats
Robot → only works

✔ No unnecessary methods
✔ Clean design

🌍 Real-World Application
Example: Multi-Feature App 📱

Instead of one big interface:

interface AppFeatures {
    void chat();
    void videoCall();
    void payment();
    void gaming();
}

👉 Not every app needs all features

✅ Better design:

ChatFeature
PaymentFeature
GamingFeature

👉 Apps implement only what they need

🧠 When Should You Use ISP?

Use ISP when:

✔ Interfaces are becoming too large
✔ Classes are forced to implement unused methods
✔ You see empty methods / exceptions like UnsupportedOperationException
✔ You want clean and modular APIs

⚙️ Benefits
✅ Cleaner code
✅ Better readability
✅ Easy maintenance
✅ Less bugs
✅ More flexibility
⚠️ Common Mistake

👉 Creating “God Interface” (very big interface)

Bad sign:

Too many methods
Not all classes use all methods

👉 Always break into smaller interfaces

🎯 Interview One-Liner

👉 “Interfaces should be specific so that implementing classes are not forced to implement methods they do not use.”

⚡ Memory Trick

👉 ISP = Don’t force unnecessary methods

